  "query_preview": "..\nLingering cloud cover has quickly dissipated out of the area \nthis morning with cumulus clouds beginning to form over the\nWhite and Chiricahua Mountains. Moisture that began to influx \ninto the area last night continues, with satellite derived PWATs\naround 0.8 to 1.1 inches across the area with the greatest \nvalues from the Catalinas eastward. Surface dewpoints reflect \nthis influx with values around 45 to 55 degrees. Thunderstorms \ntoday will start around noon to 1 PM MST initially on the White \nand Chiricahua mountains. Steering flow is very minimal around 3\nto 5 mph thus thunderstorms will mainly develop from converging\noutflow winds. While there is moisture at the surface and \naloft, enough to initiate storm development, there is not enough\nmoisture for flash flooding to be a big concern. 4 hr maximum \nHREF 30kt (35 mph) wind gust probabilities have a bullseye of \n50-90% from the Catalina's eastward with DCAPE around 1300-1500 \nJ/kg. Therefore, strong winds and blowing dust are the main \nconcerns associated with thunderstorms this afternoon.\n\nTomorrow through the week, moisture will continue to increase\nand coverage will gradually push westward. Tuesday and \nWednesday, thunderstorm chances extend from Tucson eastward with\nthe main concerns being wind and blowing dust. Extreme heat is \nalso a concern with an Extreme Heat Warning in effect from \nTuesday morning through Wednesday afternoon for areas Tucson \nnorthwestward. Moderate to Major HeatRisk is expected meaning \nthe heat will affect anyone without adequate cooling and/or \nhydration. High temperatures will range between 107 to 111 \ndegrees today through Thursday. \n\nThursday and Friday, moisture continues to push westward and \nchances for thunderstorms push into central Pima county. With \nmore deep moisture in place for areas east of Tucson, flash \nflooding begins to become a concern. Greatest risk for flash \nflooding will be contained to the mountains and associated with \nstrong thunderstorms that produce brief heavy rainfall. By the \nweekend, chances for thunderstorms extends into western Pima \ncounty with the main impacts being wind and dust for areas west \nof Tucson and wind and flash flooding for areas Tucson eastward.",
